WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:09.470
When China and Britain clashed in the first and second Opium Wars, it was over much more than the illegal drug for which these conflicts are named.

2
00:00:09.472 --> 00:00:23.326
The two nineteenth—century disputes shaped the history of modern China, leading to the downfall of its last ruling dynasty and exposing the nation to Western influences and economic domination.

3
00:00:23.327 --> 00:00:33.552
A century and a half later, China was still dealing with the consequences of the wars when the city of Hong Kong was at last returned to its control.

4
00:00:33.553 --> 00:00:43.937
In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, China was the greatest power in Asia. The emperors of the Qing dynasty had ruled since 1644.

5
00:00:43.938 --> 00:00:49.696
This was also a time of great expansion in trade and conquest by European nations.

6
00:00:49.697 --> 00:01:01.689
The Spanish controlled the Philippines; the Portuguese were established in Macau, an island on China’s southern coast; and the British had conquered China’s oldest and largest neighbor, India.

7
00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.966
But China was closed to Europe.

8
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:12.134
China was a vast nation with a self—sufficient economy, and the Qing government saw little reason to open its doors to the West.

9
00:01:12.135 --> 00:01:22.191
In 1793, the emperor told an ambassador from Britain seeking increased trade that China had no need for Britain’s manufactured goods.

10
00:01:22.192 --> 00:01:32.616
International trade was allowed only at the port of Guangzhou, then called Canton, where foreign merchants were restricted to a small area outside the city.

11
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:38.996
They were subject to Chinese law and could trade only with designated Chinese merchants.

12
00:01:38.997 --> 00:01:48.313
These restrictive trade policies were a deliberate effort to limit Chinese contact with foreigners and to protect the country from Western influence.

13
00:01:48.314 --> 00:01:58.410
The Canton system, as China’s trading policy was known in the 1700s, was strictly enforced, and it led to a trade imbalance in China’s favor.

14
00:01:58.411 --> 00:02:07.144
China was selling huge quantities of porcelain, silk, and especially tea to European nations, but was buying little in return.

15
00:02:07.145 --> 00:02:16.582
Britain imported millions of kilograms of tea from China every year, but 90 percent of the material brought to China on British ships was silver.

16
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:20.342
The trade imbalance was depleting Britain’s silver reserves.

17
00:02:20.343 --> 00:02:26.394
In a calculated effort to reverse this trend, Britain had begun exporting opium to China.

18
00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:31.009
Opium is a highly addictive drug made from the seeds of the poppy plant.

19
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:37.881
The Chinese grew a very small amount of opium, and its use was limited mainly to the upper classes.

20
00:02:37.882 --> 00:02:47.321
Nonetheless, it was recognized as a harmful substance, and Chinese emperors had passed laws against its use as early as 1729.

21
00:02:47.322 --> 00:02:53.559
Britain had a ready supply of the drug in its Indian colonies, where poppies were grown and harvested.

22
00:02:53.560 --> 00:03:10.043
In 1773, the British East India Company began a policy of selling opium to British traders, and by the end of the eighteenth century, Britain had increased exports of opium from two hundred chests a year to nearly four thousand.

23
00:03:10.044 --> 00:03:20.690
From 1796 to 1799, and again in 1810, the Chinese government extended its laws against the use, production, and sale of opium.

24
00:03:20.691 --> 00:03:30.694
The British ignored these laws and continued to bring the drug to the shores of Guangzhou. By 1830, opium addiction was a major problem in China.

25
00:03:30.695 --> 00:03:37.462
Most men under the age of forty smoked opium regularly, including many members of the Chinese army.

26
00:03:37.463 --> 00:03:45.565
Britain’s trade deficit with China had reversed to become a trade surplus, and silver was now flowing from China to England.

27
00:03:45.566 --> 00:03:53.614
The Chinese government was extremely concerned about both the addiction and the corruption that the opium trade was causing among its officials. In

28
00:03:53.615 --> 00:04:03.981
March of 1839, the emperor sent Lin Zexu, an official renowned for his integrity, to Guangzhou to enforce the ban on opium. In

29
00:04:03.982 --> 00:04:11.039
two months, he made 1,600 arrests and confiscated 11,000 pounds of opium. When

30
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:25.075
Lin seized another twenty thousand chests of opium, destroyed them, and threw them into the sea, the British saw this as unlawful destruction of their property and opened hostilities, beginning the first Opium War. The

31
00:04:25.076 --> 00:04:31.423
British navy was the greatest military power in the world, and China’s navy was quickly defeated. The

32
00:04:31.424 --> 00:04:42.720
Treaty of Nanjing ended the war in 1842, and its terms were so unfavorable to China that historians consider it the first of what they call the "Unequal Treaties." The

33
00:04:42.721 --> 00:04:56.879
treaty forced China to open four more ports to foreign trade (Fuzhou, Xiamen, Ningbo, and Shanghai), to pay a fine of $21 million in silver, and to give the island of Hong Kong to the British. The

34
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.997
restrictive Canton system was replaced by new trading rules that favored the British. Trading

35
00:05:02.998 --> 00:05:09.940
in opium, however, was still illegal. Nevertheless, Britain continued to export the drug to China. By

36
00:05:09.941 --> 00:05:18.083
the outbreak of the second Opium War, shipments of opium to China had reached a staggering seventy thousand chests per year. Under

37
00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:27.315
the new trade laws, British nationals were exempt from Chinese law, so shipowners wanting to evade the law registered their boats with the British. In

38
00:05:27.316 --> 00:05:37.747
1856, Chinese officials arrested the crew of the Arrow , a boat suspected of piracy and smuggling but registered with British authorities in Hong Kong.

39
00:05:37.748 --> 00:05:44.913
Britain took advantage of the situation to accuse the Chinese of meddling in British affairs and attacked Guangzhou.

40
00:05:44.914 --> 00:05:53.623
France joined Britain in this second Opium War, also known as the Arrow War, and two years later, a new Unequal Treaty was signed.

41
00:05:53.624 --> 00:06:03.497
The Treaty of Tianjin required China to pay new fines, open up new ports, legalize the opium trade, and give Britain further rights in China.

42
00:06:03.498 --> 00:06:14.035
Disputes over the treaty continued until 1860, finally ending when Franco—British forces marched on Beijing and set fire to the emperor’s Summer Palace.

43
00:06:14.036 --> 00:06:17.321
The Chinese had been defeated and humiliated.

44
00:06:17.322 --> 00:06:28.765
The effects of the Opium Wars on China were far—reaching. The Unequal Treaties weakened the power of the Qing government and led to internal rebellions and the dynasty’s eventual demise.

45
00:06:28.766 --> 00:06:38.638
Britain, France, Russia, Germany, and Japan were all granted control over vast Chinese erritories in an arrangement called "spheres of influence."

46
00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:46.130
Hong Kong and the Kowloon Peninsula were ceded to Britain, and Russia gained a large amount of territory in northern China.

47
00:06:46.131 --> 00:06:51.893
The country’s once strong and self—sufficient economy was brought to the point of collapse.

48
00:06:51.894 --> 00:06:58.663
The Opium Wars, however, were not just about opium and silver, but about power and the race to rule the world.

49
00:06:58.664 --> 00:07:10.107
China represented the last prize in the Far East for European countries, and these wars were carefully designed steps meant to "open" China—against the country’s will, if necessary.

50
00:07:10.108 --> 00:07:17.671
The long—lasting humiliation caused by the Unequal Treaties gave rise to a strong nationalism among Chinese people.

51
00:07:17.672 --> 00:07:27.150
This nationalism, resentment of the West, and the hardships people suffered adapting to an industrial economy helped bring China’s communist party to power.

52
00:07:27.152 --> 00:07:35.691
In 1949, the communist leader Mao Zedong declared the formation of the People’s Republic of China, as the country is known today.

